WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:02.268
-Our next guest is
an Academy Award

00:00:02.268 --> 00:00:03.302
and Grammy winner,

00:00:03.302 --> 00:00:04.603
a Rock and Roll Hall of Fame
inductee,

00:00:04.603 --> 00:00:06.739
and former frontman
of Talking Heads.

00:00:06.739 --> 00:00:09.275
His latest work,
"Here Lies Love,"

00:00:09.275 --> 00:00:11.711
is playing at the
Broadway Theatre on Broadway.

00:00:11.711 --> 00:00:14.280
Please welcome back to the show
our friend David Byrne.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:16.282
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:16.282 --> 00:00:27.293 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:27.293 --> 00:00:29.695
-Welcome back.
-Thank you!

00:00:29.695 --> 00:00:30.896
-I'm so happy you're here.
-[ Laughs ]

00:00:30.896 --> 00:00:31.964
I'm glad to be here.

00:00:31.964 --> 00:00:33.967
-And I know every time
you're here,

00:00:33.967 --> 00:00:35.601
and it's the way you get
everywhere in New York City,

00:00:35.601 --> 00:00:37.169
you biked here today.
-Yeah.

00:00:37.169 --> 00:00:38.471
It's locked outside.

00:00:38.471 --> 00:00:41.007
So, you, David Byrne,
bike to 30 Rock,

00:00:41.007 --> 00:00:42.708
and then you just
locked it outside?

00:00:42.708 --> 00:00:44.477
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:44.477 --> 00:00:47.112
-And how recognizable
are you to people

00:00:47.112 --> 00:00:48.915
as David Byrne on a bike?

00:00:48.915 --> 00:00:51.617
-Most of the time, no,
but somehow they know

00:00:51.617 --> 00:00:52.852
when I'm coming to do this show.
-Yes. Interesting.

00:00:52.852 --> 00:00:56.756
'Cause you wear a helmet,
obviously.

00:00:56.756 --> 00:00:57.790
You play it safe.
-Most of the time, yeah.

00:00:57.790 --> 00:00:59.992
-Okay. Most of the time.
-Yeah, most.

00:00:59.992 --> 00:01:01.760
-Have you --
Is the biking a new thing,

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:03.830
or have you always been
a New York City biker?

00:01:03.830 --> 00:01:06.198
-I've been doing it
for a long time.

00:01:06.198 --> 00:01:08.835
Used to live on the
Lower East Side back in the --

00:01:08.835 --> 00:01:10.870
-Yeah.
-A long time ago.

00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:12.171
There were hardly
any cabs down there.

00:01:12.171 --> 00:01:13.439
-Gotcha.

00:01:13.439 --> 00:01:16.776
-Hardly any cabs in SoHo.
It was like this weird,

00:01:16.776 --> 00:01:19.011
dangerous, whatever.
-So it was out of necessity

00:01:19.011 --> 00:01:20.512
that you were a biker?
-Kind of, yeah.

00:01:20.512 --> 00:01:22.782
It became really practical.
-And it's become safer.

00:01:22.782 --> 00:01:24.583
I think New York City has gotten
a little bit better for bikers,

00:01:24.583 --> 00:01:26.152
but not entirely safe.

00:01:26.152 --> 00:01:28.221
-Not entirely.
I wouldn't go to a friend

00:01:28.221 --> 00:01:29.922
and say --
who's never done it and say,

00:01:29.922 --> 00:01:31.824
"Oh, just go ahead,
just go ahead.

00:01:31.824 --> 00:01:33.025
Just jump in there."
[ Laughter ]

00:01:33.025 --> 00:01:35.628
-Because you -- I know
when you went on tour,

00:01:35.628 --> 00:01:38.965
you and your cast
would bike in other cities.

00:01:38.965 --> 00:01:40.366
-Yeah.
-So it must be nice.

00:01:40.366 --> 00:01:41.901
There must be some
American cities where you go,

00:01:41.901 --> 00:01:43.169
"Oh, one day.

00:01:43.169 --> 00:01:45.304
Wouldn't it be great
if New York was like this?"

00:01:45.304 --> 00:01:48.774
-Oh, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.
I mean, there --

00:01:48.774 --> 00:01:51.945
Austin has -- amazingly
has incredible bike lanes.

00:01:51.945 --> 00:01:55.548
Portland, of course,
of "Portlandia," yes.

00:01:55.548 --> 00:01:57.250
Lots of bike lanes.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:57.250 --> 00:01:58.984
-They do.

00:01:58.984 --> 00:02:01.721
So, congratulations.
Your second Broadway show.

00:02:01.721 --> 00:02:03.122
-Yes.
Well, yes, it is.

00:02:03.122 --> 00:02:05.024
-As a guy who used to bike
around the Lower East Side,

00:02:05.024 --> 00:02:07.393
did you ever think you'd have
two Broadway shows?

00:02:07.393 --> 00:02:09.962
-Uh, br-br-br-br, yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:09.962 --> 00:02:12.799
-So, this is based on an album
you did with Fatboy Slim?

00:02:12.799 --> 00:02:16.034
It is a --
It's about Imelda Marcos,

00:02:16.034 --> 00:02:20.106
the wife of the former dictator
in the Philippines.

00:02:20.106 --> 00:02:22.241
-Exactly.
-And what made you first think

00:02:22.241 --> 00:02:27.013
that this would be
a good topic for -- of music?

00:02:27.013 --> 00:02:28.581
-Just cried out to me. Yes.

00:02:28.581 --> 00:02:33.085
No. I'd been to --
Way back in the day,

00:02:33.085 --> 00:02:36.222
I'd been to some discos,
but I usually just went to see

00:02:36.222 --> 00:02:39.358
if there was a musical act
performing.

00:02:39.358 --> 00:02:43.663
And the acts who had club hits
in those days

00:02:43.663 --> 00:02:47.033
would come into the disco,
stand on a little platform,

00:02:47.033 --> 00:02:50.269
put in a tape that had their
backing tracks of their song,

00:02:50.269 --> 00:02:53.406
and just sing their two hits,
and they'd be gone.

00:02:53.406 --> 00:02:56.042
And I thought,
"But what if they could do more?

00:02:56.042 --> 00:03:00.146
What if you could do
a whole evening

00:03:00.146 --> 00:03:04.683
and tell a story that way
while the audience is dancing?"

00:03:04.683 --> 00:03:07.386
I like the idea of, "What
if you can have an audience

00:03:07.386 --> 00:03:08.888
that's only half
paying attention?"

00:03:08.888 --> 00:03:12.058
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:12.058 --> 00:03:13.993
-Somehow that appealed to me.

00:03:13.993 --> 00:03:17.963
And -- because I thought,
"They'll absorb it.

00:03:17.963 --> 00:03:20.300
They'll absorb the story.
They don't have to be

00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:23.202
just paying attention
every second."

00:03:23.202 --> 00:03:25.971
Put that in my back pocket.
And then years later,

00:03:25.971 --> 00:03:31.544
I read that Imelda Marcos
loved going to clubs.

00:03:31.544 --> 00:03:35.147
She had a mirror ball installed
in her New York townhouse,

00:03:35.147 --> 00:03:36.515
which we don't all have.

00:03:36.515 --> 00:03:38.317
-No, we don't all have.
-No, not all of us.

00:03:38.317 --> 00:03:40.219
-We all should, yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:40.219 --> 00:03:46.259
-So, I googled that up and saw
a video of her dancing

00:03:46.259 --> 00:03:51.363
under her home mirror ball
with Khashoggi, the arms dealer.

00:03:51.363 --> 00:03:53.333
-Wow.
-Yeah, and I thought,

00:03:53.333 --> 00:03:58.037
"Uh, she lives in this world
that I've been thinking about

00:03:58.037 --> 00:03:59.772
if there could be
a show that way.

00:03:59.772 --> 00:04:04.143
And look who she's dancing with.
There's a story here."

00:04:04.143 --> 00:04:06.345
-So -- So it just clicks
together like that.

00:04:06.345 --> 00:04:09.015
-Yeah. So I can bring
these two ideas together,

00:04:09.015 --> 00:04:12.184
these two things together,
and let's see what happens.

00:04:12.184 --> 00:04:13.386
Let's see if there's a story.

00:04:13.386 --> 00:04:14.654
-And again, so I'm going
to show a photo.

00:04:14.654 --> 00:04:17.723
This is a Broadway theater
here in New York City.

00:04:17.723 --> 00:04:19.024
You've taken out all the seats

00:04:19.024 --> 00:04:21.627
so that the audience
on the ground floor there,

00:04:21.627 --> 00:04:23.629
in the orchestra area,
just sort of dances

00:04:23.629 --> 00:04:24.830
throughout
the course of the show.

00:04:24.830 --> 00:04:26.298
-Yes.
So, we actually --

00:04:26.298 --> 00:04:27.533
I mean, okay.

00:04:27.533 --> 00:04:29.635
If you don't want to dance,
you don't have to.

00:04:29.635 --> 00:04:31.170
There are seats.
-Yes.

00:04:31.170 --> 00:04:33.139
-There are seats.
-There's nice balcony seats,

00:04:33.139 --> 00:04:34.607
so you don't have
to be with this...

00:04:34.607 --> 00:04:38.778
-But my dream came true,
and I could do a show

00:04:38.778 --> 00:04:41.046
where a good portion
of the audience is...

00:04:41.046 --> 00:04:42.481
-Yeah.
-...able to dance.

00:04:42.481 --> 00:04:45.618
-It must make you so happy
to see that this idea you had

00:04:45.618 --> 00:04:46.985
all those years ago
is paying off.

00:04:46.985 --> 00:04:48.521
And it's
an entirely Filipino cast.

00:04:48.521 --> 00:04:50.022
That must be an exciting thing
to put together.

00:04:50.022 --> 00:04:51.323
-That's very exciting.
They're kind of telling

00:04:51.323 --> 00:04:53.292
their own -- the story
of their own country.

00:04:53.292 --> 00:04:54.693
-Uh-huh.
And do you feel like --

00:04:54.693 --> 00:04:56.329
I mean, obviously, people
who buy Broadway tickets,

00:04:56.329 --> 00:04:58.731
you know, they maybe aren't
expecting something like this.

00:04:58.731 --> 00:05:00.199
Do you feel like
you've prepared them enough

00:05:00.199 --> 00:05:02.368
for what they're about
to walk in for?

00:05:02.368 --> 00:05:03.502
-I hope so.

00:05:03.502 --> 00:05:04.804
I hope when they go
to buy the tickets,

00:05:04.804 --> 00:05:06.171
they read the thing that says

00:05:06.171 --> 00:05:08.674
"If you buy a ticket
on the dance floor,

00:05:08.674 --> 00:05:12.145
you will be standing
for an hour and a half,

00:05:12.145 --> 00:05:14.246
but you're not
gonna miss anything."

00:05:14.246 --> 00:05:15.748
It's all happening
all around you.

00:05:15.748 --> 00:05:22.054
But if you want a seat, there's
lots of ways to sit, as well.

00:05:22.054 --> 00:05:26.025
Yeah, I haven't --
I go about once a week.

00:05:26.025 --> 00:05:27.393
-That's exciting.

00:05:27.393 --> 00:05:30.329
-And I usually go on the dance
floor because I just --

00:05:30.329 --> 00:05:32.197
I feel like I'm immersed.
I'm in it, then.

00:05:32.197 --> 00:05:33.666
-Well, I feel like
you are someone

00:05:33.666 --> 00:05:36.502
who has always
seemed to be taken over by music

00:05:36.502 --> 00:05:38.204
and wanted to dance.

00:05:38.204 --> 00:05:40.239
-I always wanted to, yes.
-Yeah.

00:05:40.239 --> 00:05:41.673
That's why we --
[ Laughter ]

00:05:41.673 --> 00:05:43.475
That's why we never play music
during the interviews.

00:05:43.475 --> 00:05:45.645
Because we're afraid
you'll just wander off, yeah.

00:05:45.645 --> 00:05:46.945
[ Laughter ]

00:05:46.945 --> 00:05:48.781
I always ask people like this,
especially ones

00:05:48.781 --> 00:05:50.482
who've had such long,
iconic careers.

00:05:50.482 --> 00:05:53.386
Do you ever hear when
you're at a cafe or in a car,

00:05:53.386 --> 00:05:54.786
do you ever hear your music?

00:05:54.786 --> 00:05:57.656
And what is your reaction
when you hear songs of yours?

00:05:57.656 --> 00:05:59.926
-If I heard it,

00:05:59.926 --> 00:06:02.094
sometimes friends point it out.
I don't notice.

00:06:02.094 --> 00:06:03.595
-Really.
-I don't notice.

00:06:03.595 --> 00:06:05.297
I have it tuned out.

00:06:05.297 --> 00:06:08.501
It's like another part of my
brain or -- for musicians.

00:06:08.501 --> 00:06:10.503
They start to they listen
to music

00:06:10.503 --> 00:06:12.772
in a different way.
And my friends will point --

00:06:12.772 --> 00:06:15.607
"David, David, David,
listen to that.

00:06:15.607 --> 00:06:18.577
Do you know who that is?"
[ Laughter ]

00:06:18.577 --> 00:06:20.946
-This sounds like they're
giving you an Alzheimer's test.

00:06:20.946 --> 00:06:24.517
-Yeah!
[ Laughter ]

00:06:24.517 --> 00:06:27.386
-Now this --
We've talked about this before.

00:06:27.386 --> 00:06:28.788
For my money,
one of the best --

00:06:28.788 --> 00:06:30.790
the greatest
concert movie of all time.

00:06:30.790 --> 00:06:32.892
"Stop Making Sense"
was just re-released.

00:06:32.892 --> 00:06:34.594
They were showing it
in theaters.

00:06:34.594 --> 00:06:35.828
[ Cheers and applause ]
Remastered.

00:06:35.828 --> 00:06:37.763
-Thank you!

00:06:37.763 --> 00:06:39.264
And you went.
You watched it.

00:06:39.264 --> 00:06:41.133
-Oh, I've watched it, yeah,
now, a few times

00:06:41.133 --> 00:06:42.735
during the opening.
That was a couple weeks ago.

00:06:42.735 --> 00:06:44.937
-How many years had it been
since you've seen it?

00:06:44.937 --> 00:06:47.206
-It had been at least
10 years,

00:06:47.206 --> 00:06:51.510
maybe 15, since I'd seen it
projected on a screen.

00:06:51.510 --> 00:06:55.114
And when it got reprinted
from the mast--

00:06:55.114 --> 00:06:57.817
from the negative
and remixed and everything,

00:06:57.817 --> 00:07:01.621
I obviously went to
a screening, and I thought,

00:07:01.621 --> 00:07:03.756
"Wow, this looks great.
It sounds great,

00:07:03.756 --> 00:07:07.159
but who is that guy?"
[ Laughter ]

00:07:07.159 --> 00:07:10.062
He's a very serious young man.

00:07:10.062 --> 00:07:11.130
[ Laughter ]

00:07:11.130 --> 00:07:15.100
He's very intent.
Is he having any fun?

00:07:15.100 --> 00:07:16.335
And --
-[ Laughs ]

00:07:16.335 --> 00:07:17.870
[ Laughter ]

00:07:17.870 --> 00:07:19.872
-It is -- I will say,
because obviously --

00:07:19.872 --> 00:07:22.975
I've met this era David Byrne
is who I met.

00:07:22.975 --> 00:07:24.643
And you were not
who I thought you'd be

00:07:24.643 --> 00:07:26.378
based on that era David Byrne,

00:07:26.378 --> 00:07:27.780
who seemed like
he had a sense of humor

00:07:27.780 --> 00:07:31.517
because there was something
so theatrical about the film

00:07:31.517 --> 00:07:34.053
and the performance that I --
You know, look, giant suit.

00:07:34.053 --> 00:07:35.755
That's really funny.
You know what I mean?

00:07:35.755 --> 00:07:37.022
-Yes.
-I feel like a serious guy

00:07:37.022 --> 00:07:38.256
doesn't wear a giant suit

00:07:38.256 --> 00:07:40.759
unless he knows there's
something funny about it.

00:07:40.759 --> 00:07:41.994
But were you having fun?

00:07:41.994 --> 00:07:43.696
Like, when you -- I mean,
I know it doesn't look --

00:07:43.696 --> 00:07:45.964
But was your memory of it
before you saw it, do you think,

00:07:45.964 --> 00:07:48.434
"Oh, my God,
that was so much fun to do"?

00:07:48.434 --> 00:07:51.103
-Yes. At the end --

00:07:51.103 --> 00:07:52.371
As the show progresses,

00:07:52.371 --> 00:07:54.206
I do loosen up,
and I'm having fun,

00:07:54.206 --> 00:07:57.042
and the music is just --
I'm just carried away by it.

00:07:57.042 --> 00:07:59.812
So, yes,
I am having fun, but...

00:07:59.812 --> 00:08:02.548
I'm also,
like a lot of performers,

00:08:02.548 --> 00:08:04.283
I'm thinking, "Okay.
What do I do next?

00:08:04.283 --> 00:08:05.484
What happens next?

00:08:05.484 --> 00:08:08.554
Where am I supposed to be?
What's going on?"

00:08:08.554 --> 00:08:10.389
Yeah, there's a little bit
of that going on.

00:08:10.389 --> 00:08:12.591
-And was it different -- or, was
there a heightened sense of that

00:08:12.591 --> 00:08:14.560
because you knew, "This time
we're putting it on film"?

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:16.195
Obviously, you've performed
those songs before,

00:08:16.195 --> 00:08:19.264
but did you know the whole time,
"There's cameras here.

00:08:19.264 --> 00:08:20.966
This is the one for..."?

00:08:20.966 --> 00:08:23.035
-[ Inhales sharply ]

00:08:23.035 --> 00:08:24.603
Uh...
yeah, but then, after a while,

00:08:24.603 --> 00:08:25.837
you have to just
kind of go with it.

00:08:25.837 --> 00:08:28.207
You just have to go,
like, "We're doing a show.

00:08:28.207 --> 00:08:31.043
I'm playing to the audience,
not to the cameras."

00:08:31.043 --> 00:08:33.979
Yeah.
-We did an episode based on this

00:08:33.979 --> 00:08:35.147
starring Fred Armisen.

00:08:35.147 --> 00:08:37.416
Fred wrote it,
called "Final Transmission."

00:08:37.416 --> 00:08:39.518
And did you know --
-[ Laughs ]

00:08:39.518 --> 00:08:41.754
-So here's you guys.

00:08:41.754 --> 00:08:45.157
And, uh -- and then
here's Fred and Bill Hader.

00:08:45.157 --> 00:08:47.793
And there's Maya Rudolph.
There's Bill Hader.

00:08:47.793 --> 00:08:50.362
Did you know Fred
had a David Byrne impression?

00:08:50.362 --> 00:08:52.397
-Yes, I did.
[ Laughter ]

00:08:52.397 --> 00:08:55.033
But I --
I found out by accident.

00:08:55.033 --> 00:08:57.803
I went to a fairly small club
downtown

00:08:57.803 --> 00:09:01.139
to see a musical act
at one point,

00:09:01.139 --> 00:09:05.578
and Fred was the opening act.
And I'm there in the club,

00:09:05.578 --> 00:09:08.781
and I'm watching him do me.
And I --

00:09:08.781 --> 00:09:10.883
-By the way, so doing comedy
before a music show

00:09:10.883 --> 00:09:12.251
is one of the hardest
things in the world.

00:09:12.251 --> 00:09:13.919
The only person I've ever seen
do it well is Fred.

00:09:13.919 --> 00:09:15.788
-Fred can do it.
-Because Fred does --

00:09:15.788 --> 00:09:17.122
He is a musician, as well.
-Yeah.

00:09:17.122 --> 00:09:20.292
-And so Fred does exactly
what a music crowd wants to see.

00:09:20.292 --> 00:09:21.960
Because a comedy --
if you went to a comedy club

00:09:21.960 --> 00:09:23.262
and did a David Byrne
impression,

00:09:23.262 --> 00:09:25.297
it might be a crazy bad idea.

00:09:25.297 --> 00:09:26.866
But you do a David Byrne
impression

00:09:26.866 --> 00:09:28.400
at a comedy s--
at a rock show,

00:09:28.400 --> 00:09:29.535
it might actually work.

00:09:29.535 --> 00:09:30.702
But he didn't know
you were there?

00:09:30.702 --> 00:09:32.337
-He did other things, too,

00:09:32.337 --> 00:09:35.107
but he didn't know I was there.
I'm watching.

00:09:35.107 --> 00:09:38.310
I'm thinking, "He's doing me."
[ Laughter ]

00:09:38.310 --> 00:09:39.879
"And he's good at it."

00:09:39.879 --> 00:09:41.980
[ Laughter ]

00:09:41.980 --> 00:09:44.283
After the thing --
He didn't know I was there.

00:09:44.283 --> 00:09:45.751
-Yeah.
-He came out.

00:09:45.751 --> 00:09:46.852
I'm still in the audience.

00:09:46.852 --> 00:09:49.088
He goes,
"David, I'm really sorry."

00:09:49.088 --> 00:09:51.156
[ Laughter ]

00:09:51.156 --> 00:09:54.026
"I hope that didn't bother you
too much."

00:09:54.026 --> 00:09:55.961
No, I just said --
-You should have told him,

00:09:55.961 --> 00:09:57.696
like, one of my friends
called me and told me

00:09:57.696 --> 00:09:59.364
you were doing it,
and I came down..."

00:09:59.364 --> 00:10:02.701
[ Laughter ]
"...to confront you."

00:10:02.701 --> 00:10:04.303
Well, Fred is not alone.

00:10:04.303 --> 00:10:06.238
We're all such huge fans
of yours.

00:10:06.238 --> 00:10:08.441
It was so cool to pay homage
to that wonderful film,

00:10:08.441 --> 00:10:09.775
and I'm so glad people
could see it again.

00:10:09.775 --> 00:10:11.410
And it's just so lovely
to have you here.

00:10:11.410 --> 00:10:13.913
Two Broadway shows.
You're finally making good.

00:10:13.913 --> 00:10:15.881
You're finally making good.

00:10:15.881 --> 00:10:16.916
David Byrne, everybody.

00:10:16.916 --> 00:10:19.451
"Here Lies Love" is exceptional.

00:10:19.451 --> 00:10:20.719
It's playing
at the Broadway Theatre.

00:10:20.719 --> 00:10:22.588
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

